STEP 5 -- unauthenticated client gets a lease, then logs in at 192.168.60.1     NOT MEASURED (login); lease + reachability measured
  5a lease, 08:04-08:06, `dhcp4 client eth6 --mac 00:90:0b:2a:11:c3 --wait 10`:
  | try1 OFFER: NONE (first DISCOVER unanswered, as run 2 step 4a)
  | try2 OFFER 192.168.60.2 lease_time=60; ACK 192.168.60.2 lease_time=60 renewal=10 rebinding=12
  | (lease expired; re-leased 08:06) try4 NONE, try5 ACK 192.168.60.3 lease_time=60
  show auth supplicant brief: port1.0.81  1    W    0090.0b2a.11c3 Connecting        192.168.60.3    --

  5b route (approved): `tools/tb sudotool tb5 wa1030 hostnet route60-add` (work/run3-route60-add.out):
  | ip route add 192.168.60.0/24 via 192.168.50.1 dev eth6 -> rc 2 Error: Nexthop has invalid gateway.
  | RESULT route60-add: NOT AS ASKED   (route absent before and after)
  Cause, measured (tools/tb tool tb5 wa1030 hostinfo, work/run3-tb5-hostinfo.out): tb5 eth6 (00:90:0b:2a:11:c3,
  UP) now holds 10.37.5.129/27 + fd32:b1f0:dff7:506::1/64, NOT 192.168.50.10/24 as the queue records; new
  macvlans jm1..jm5@eth6 (DOWN, 192.168.50.11-15/24). 192.168.50.1 is therefore not on-link and the route
  cannot be installed. Not changed by this tester; not repaired (root on tb5). Sent to the sentinel as
  NEEDS TEST ENGINEER (08:05).

  5c what the leased address DOES get, measured without any host address (scapy from eth6, MAC c3, src 192.168.60.3):
  arpleg r3syn / r3syn2 (08:06): SYN to 192.168.60.1:80 x4, ICMP x4, then SYN to 60.1:80, 50.1:80, 8.8.8.8:80,
    60.1:443 x3 each -> no reply rows (no ARP had resolved: the answerer saw no request in its window).
  arpleg r3syn3 (08:07, with --arp 192.168.60.1) + a sniff-only leg filtered on the DUT's MAC (r3sniff):
  | x3  ARP-REQ  who-has 192.168.60.1 tell 192.168.60.3          [eth 00:90:0b:2a:11:c3 -> broadcast]
  | x2  ARP-REPLY 192.168.60.1 is-at e0:1a:ea:d5:04:56            [eth e0:1a:ea:d5:04:56 -> 00:90:0b:2a:11:c3]
  | x1  ARP-REQ  who-has 192.168.60.3 tell 192.168.50.1           [eth e0:1a:ea:d5:04:56 -> broadcast]
  | x1  ARP-REPLY 192.168.60.3 is-at 00:90:0b:2a:11:c3            (our answerer)
  | x4  TCP 192.168.60.1:80 -> 192.168.60.3:40000 SA              [eth e0:1a:ea:d5:04:56 -> 00:90:0b:2a:11:c3]
  | x3  ICMP echo 192.168.60.3 -> 192.168.60.1: no reply
  => The DUT owns 192.168.60.1 (ARP), and its web-auth server ACCEPTS a TCP connection on port 80 from the
     unauthenticated client's LEASED address (SYN -> SYN-ACK). ICMP to 60.1 is not answered while unauthenticated.
     Observation: the DUT's ARP request for the leased client is sourced from 192.168.50.1 (vlan1), not 60.1.
  DUT after (work/run3-s5-dut-b.out): ARP 192.168.60.3 0090.0b2a.11c3 vlan1 port1.0.81 dynamic; the W
  Connecting entry had aged out.

  NOT MEASURED: the HTTP login itself from the leased address (wrong password -> supplicant not authorized,
  dataplane 0/4; right password -> Authenticated, 4/4). Why: tb5 has no host address/route that can source
  HTTP from (or to) 192.168.60.0/24: the approved route needs 192.168.50.1 on-link on eth6, and eth6 no longer
  holds 192.168.50.x. A login from eth6's own address is not possible either for the same reason. Not widened.
  (Option for the Test Engineer, not done: a userspace scapy TCP client from 192.168.60.x -- the kernel does
  not own that address so it would not RST -- would need a new tool run as root on tb5.)

